Portability fixes for mo_yanxi_vulkan_wrapper, applied by build.mcpp.

All are upstream bugs rather than xrgui's, and all belong in
Yuria-Shikibe/mo_yanxi_vulkan_wrapper; this file keeps the tree buildable off
MSVC until they are.

Two kinds, and they are not the same kind:

  (a) NON-PORTABLE CODE, rewritten to standard C++. Nothing is conditional and
      MSVC gets the same fix -- in two of the three, a real defect goes with it.
  (b) STANDARD CODE a library has not caught up with. The code is right; libc++
      has not shipped the facility. Those route through the mo_yanxi.views /
      mo_yanxi.functional shims that mcpp/mo_yanxi_utility adds, and revert to
      `std::` the day the #if in those files stops choosing the fallback.

(a) --------------------------------------------------------------------------

util/exception.ixx -- three types moved from std::exception to
std::runtime_error. `std::exception(const char*)` is an MSVC extension; the
standard type has no such constructor, so this did not compile on clang or GCC
at all. The three what() overrides went with it: each only forwarded to the
base, and each was declared without noexcept over a noexcept base, which is
separately ill-formed. It also removes a real defect -- the argument was
`msg.data()` on a std::string_view, not guaranteed NUL-terminated, so a view
over part of a larger buffer produced a message that ran past its end.

vk.vertex_consteval_def.ixx -- binding_class_finder rewritten from a
conditional_t recursion to one `if constexpr` function. std::conditional_t
instantiates BOTH arms, so the tail was evaluated even when the head attribute
already matched; and because the attributes are non-type parameters of
different types, deducing the one-attribute partial specialisation against a
longer list made clang try to convert one attribute type to another. `if
constexpr` discards the untaken branch, so neither arises.

objects/physical_device.ixx -- two enumerate loops became index loops. One
discarded the element and used only the index, so the adaptor bought nothing;
the other is in a namespace that already has its own `enumerate`, so the
unqualified name was taken.

stack_trace.ixx -- the no-<stacktrace> fallback took one argument while its only
caller passes two. The branch that exists FOR a library without <stacktrace> did
not compile on one.

vk.util.ixx -- `std::ranges::const_iterator_t<R>` -> `iterator_t<const R>`.
const_iterator_t is P2278, which libc++ has not shipped. Both uses are in a
`requires requires(...)` declaring a `value` parameter the body never mentions
(the one line that did is commented out), so this changes what the constraint
SPELLS and not what it accepts for any const-iterable range.

stack_trace.cpp -- `import` hoisted out of `#if defined(__cpp_lib_stacktrace)`.
mcpp's M1 scanner rejects an import inside a conditional preprocessor block,
even an inactive one; importing unconditionally is harmless. NOT a
`[scan_overrides]` declaration: that was tried and mcpp's audit rejected it,
because an implementation unit also imports the module it belongs to AND
whether meta_programming is imported depends on the macro -- so no fixed
declaration is true on both MSVC and clang.

(b) --------------------------------------------------------------------------

cmd/cmd.render.ixx, objects/resources/{descriptor_buffer,command_buffer}.ixx,
stack_trace.cpp -- `std::views::enumerate` -> `mo_yanxi::views::enumerate`, and
`std::move_only_function` -> `mo_yanxi::move_only_function`.

