+
+// The I-th type in a pack. Types only -- no attribute VALUE is ever a template
+// argument here, and that distinction is the whole fix.
+//
+// Every earlier form passed `Attrs...` (the values) to some other template, and
+// merely writing that made clang try to convert one attribute to another:
+//
+//   error: no viable conversion from 'const vertex_attribute<float[3], ...>'
+//          to 'vertex_attribute<float[2], ...>'
+//
+// The callee's body was never involved: a conditional_t recursion, an
+// `if constexpr` recursion, a plain index loop, `std::tuple{Attrs...}` and a
+// one-attribute-at-a-time helper all failed identically, and all of them
+// compile clean when reduced to a standalone file on this same compiler.
+//
+// What DOES work is visible three lines below this comment's use: the existing
+// static_assert already writes `typename decltype(Attrs)::class_type` in a fold
+// and always has. So `decltype(Attrs)` -- the TYPE -- crosses the boundary
+// fine, and `Attrs` -- the value -- does not. The mechanism behind that is NOT
+// established; the rule for clang + C++20 modules in this codebase is to
+// replace the shape, and this is the narrowest replacement that fits it.
+template <std::size_t I, typename First, typename... Rest>
+struct nth_type {
+    using type = typename nth_type<I - 1, Rest...>::type;
 };
 
-template <std::uint32_t Binding, auto LastAttr>
